    <body>
      <sec id="article-28519.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Rhinitis medicamentosa, commonly known as rebound congestion, is a drug-induced form of nonallergic rhinitis caused by prolonged or inappropriate use of topical nasal decongestants. These medications are widely used for symptomatic relief of nasal congestion associated with allergic rhinitis, acute and chronic rhinosinusitis, upper respiratory tract infections, and nasal polyps, and are readily available over the counter. Although effective in the short term, prolonged use can worsen nasal obstruction, cause mucosal edema, and lead to dependence, often complicating diagnosis and management.</p>
        <p>Rhinitis medicamentosa remains underrecognized in clinical practice because of nonspecific symptoms, limited patient awareness of decongestant overuse, and likely underreporting. Effective management requires timely identification, patient education, discontinuation of the offending agent, and appropriate use of adjunctive therapies such as intranasal and oral corticosteroids. This activity reviews the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, evaluation, and management of rhinitis medicamentosa, with an emphasis on prevention and patient counseling. The role of the interprofessional healthcare team, including clinicians, pharmacists, and nursing staff, is highlighted to improve recognition, treatment adherence, and patient outcomes in this common yet preventable condition.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the symptoms or presentation of a patient with rhinitis medicamentosa.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate rhinitis medicamentosa from other potential causes.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement current evidence-based, individualized management options for patients suspected of having rhinitis medicamentosa.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement effective interprofessional strategies to improve care and outcomes for patients with rhinitis medicamentosa.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=28519&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=28519">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Rhinitis medicamentosa (RM), commonly referred to as rebound congestion, is a condition characterized by inflammation of the nasal mucosa caused by excessive use of topical nasal decongestants. Symptoms of RM may include nasal congestion, runny nose (rhinorrhea), sneezing, nasal itching, and postnasal drip. This type of rhinitis can occur alongside allergic and other nonallergic forms.</p>
        <p>To accurately diagnose RM, a thorough patient history and a physical examination are essential. Both clinicians and patients need to recognize that certain medications can lead to rhinitis. Despite warnings on prescriptions and manufacturer labels about the potential for dependence associated with some nasal decongestants, drug-induced rhinitis often goes undiagnosed. Increasing awareness of this condition is crucial for achieving more accurate diagnoses and effective treatment. Ideally, the offending medication should be discontinued or replaced.</p>
        <p>RM is classified as a subset of drug-induced rhinitis specifically caused by the prolonged overuse of topical nasal decongestants, which leads to a distinct mechanism of congestion. Histological analysis of the sinus mucosa in cases of RM typically shows ciliary loss, damaged epithelium, infiltration of inflammatory cells, and edema. The nasal mucosa may appear swollen and erythematous, with punctate bleeding and minimal mucus production.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Topical decongestants are typically used to relieve nasal congestion associated with allergic rhinitis, acute or chronic rhinosinusitis, nasal polyps, or upper respiratory tract infections. The earliest nasal decongestants were primarily derived from ephedrine, and reports of rebound congestion after prolonged use were common. This phenomenon was observed to develop as early as 3 days and as late as 4 to 6 weeks of use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;However, with the emergence of modern vasoconstrictors, such as the imidazoline derivatives, the risk of developing rhinitis medicamentosa appears lower than with earlier generations of nasal decongestants, though it remains clinically significant with prolonged or inappropriate use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Rhinitis medicamentosa, also called rebound or chemical rhinitis, results from prolonged or improper use of intranasal vasoconstrictors such as oxymetazoline, phenylephrine, xylometazoline, and cocaine, as well as oral &#x003b2;-adrenoceptor antagonists, antipsychotics, oral contraceptives, and antihypertensives. However, the mechanisms by which topical nasal decongestants and oral medications cause congestion differ. Typically, RM develops after 5 to 7 days of topical decongestant use. As effectiveness declines, patients often increase the dose and frequency of application. Several conditions can lead to decongestant overuse, including upper respiratory infections, allergic and nonallergic rhinitis, nasal obstructions such as a deviated septum, enlarged inferior turbinates, nasal polyps, rhinosinusitis, pregnancy, and the use of supplemental oxygen, continuous positive airway pressure, or&#x000a0;bilevel positive airway pressure for sleep apnea. Patients often overlook the importance of careful use because symptoms worsen despite continued use, especially in cases of severe nasal congestion.</p>
        <p>Topical intranasal decongestants&#x000a0;(adrenoreceptor activity)<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>are categorized as follows:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>&#x003b2;-Phenylethylamine derivatives mimic the effects of the sympathetic nervous system by producing vasoconstriction via activation of &#x003b1;1 adrenergic receptors. Rebound vasodilation may occur due to low &#x003b2;&#x02013;adrenergic receptor affinity.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Ephedrine hydrochloride (&#x003b1;1, &#x003b1;2, &#x003b2;1, &#x003b2;2)&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Phenylephrine hydrochloride (&#x003b1;1)&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Imidazolines, on the other hand, produce their effect mainly via &#x003b1;2-adrenergic receptor agonism. This difference in adrenoreceptor sensitivity makes imidazoline agents more effective at reducing mucosal blood flow, owing to their vasoconstrictive effects on both capacitance and resistance vessels in the nasal mucosa. The imidazole group shows a more potent, longer-acting effect. For example, 0.1% xylometazoline hydrochloride acts within 5 min and lasts up to 10 hours, whereas 1% phenylephrine acts within 15 to 20 min and lasts 2 to 4 hours.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Naphazoline hydrochloride</p></list-item><list-item><p>Oxymetazoline hydrochloride</p></list-item><list-item><p>Xylometazoline hydrochloride</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Benzalkonium chloride (BKC) is a widely used antimicrobial preservative in topical nasal decongestants. Findings from studies indicated that BKC may worsen rhinitis medicamentosa, possibly due to increased mucosal swelling.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r6">[6]</xref>.&#x000a0;However, evidence regarding its clinical significance is mixed because worsening nasal congestion has not been consistently demonstrated among patients using intranasal corticosteroid formulations containing BKC.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The exact incidence of RM is not well known and is likely underreported. Underreporting&#x000a0;occurs mainly because patients often do not provide sufficient information about their use of topical decongestants. Many people mistakenly believe that using larger amounts of these medications will more effectively relieve severe nasal congestion during upper respiratory illnesses. Among those who self-medicate for persistent rhinitis, the overuse of intranasal decongestants is quite common, affecting nearly half of patients (49%), despite general awareness of the recommended limits on usage duration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;RM may be more common in younger patients, but the condition does not appear to have a predilection for race or sex. Although the precise mechanism of RM is not clearly defined, it may be regarded as a form of addiction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;The relationship between RM and addictive substances is unclear, but many patients without a history of addiction may overuse topical intranasal decongestants, depending on their underlying condition.</p>
        <p>RM accounts for approximately 14% of cases of nonallergic rhinitis in the general population, although reported prevalence varies across studies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r10">[10]</xref> In results from a cross-sectional study of the Dutch population, chronic rhinitis affected 40% of individuals, with nonallergic rhinitis comprising 65% of cases. Rhinitis medicamentosa was the second most common nonallergic phenotype (14%), after idiopathic rhinitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Risk factors for rhinitis medicamentosa include smoking, anxiety disorders, and underlying nasal inflammation. Affected individuals are more likely to smoke, have elevated anxiety scores, and show inflammatory changes on nasal cytology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Physiology of Nasal Congestion&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>The nasal mucosal vascular system consists of resistance vessels, such as small arteries, arterioles, and arteriovenous anastomoses, that are mainly regulated by &#x003b1;2-adrenergic receptors. The system also includes capacitance vessels, such as the venous plexus, which are regulated by both &#x003b1;1- and &#x003b1;2-adrenergic receptors. These venous plexuses are densely innervated by sympathetic fibers, which release norepinephrine upon stimulation. This neurotransmitter binds to prejunctional &#x003b1;2 and postjunctional &#x003b1;1 and &#x003b1;2 receptors, leading to vasoconstriction of the large venous sinusoids and collecting veins. As a result, blood flow decreases, helping to reduce nasal edema and rhinorrhea.</p>
        <p>Factors contributing to nasal congestion include stimulation of the parasympathetic nervous system, activation of C-fibers, and nonadrenergic, noncholinergic peptidergic nerves. The release of mediators from mast cells, eosinophils, and basophils can also trigger the release of histamine, tryptase, kinins, prostaglandins, and leukotrienes, which together influence nasal resistance, vascular dynamics, and capacitance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Plasma exudation occurs through superficial capillaries, and goblet cells increase mucin production. Local mediators also affect nasal congestion by altering nasal resistance and vascular capacitance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>RM may be secondary to decreased endogenous sympathetic norepinephrine production via negative feedback.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r15">[15]</xref> With prolonged use or following discontinuation, the sympathetic nerves may be unable to maintain vasoconstriction because norepinephrine release is suppressed. Several hypotheses have been proposed regarding rhinitis medicamentosa, although the exact pathophysiology remains unknown:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hypothesis 1:</bold> Chronic vasoconstriction leads to ischemia of the nasal mucosa, predisposing it to interstitial edema.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hypothesis 2:</bold> Fatigue of the constrictor mechanisms occurs, resulting in reactive hyperemia and congestion; this subsequently correlates with reduced sensitivity to endogenous catecholamines, with adrenoreceptors becoming refractory to nasal decongestants and necessitating higher medication dosages (tachyphylaxis).</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hypothesis 3:</bold> Changes in vasomotor tone lead to increased vascular permeability and edema.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hypothesis 4:</bold> &#x003b2;-Adrenoreceptor activity may outlast the &#x003b1; effects, leading to rebound vasodilation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r17">[17]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Multiple histological changes&#x000a0;have been described in rhinitis medicamentosa based on animal studies, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Nasociliary loss and changes in nasociliary structure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Squamous cell metaplasia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Increased mucus production</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Epithelial cells can change from ciliated columnar to nonciliated, stratified squamous epithelium.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Epithelial cell denudation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Increased intercellular widening, vascularity, fibrosis, and edema of the epithelial cell layer</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Goblet cell hyperplasia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Increase in epidermal growth factor receptor in the epithelial cell layer</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Increase in lymphocytes, fibroblasts, and plasma cells</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>However, results in human studies have been inconclusive.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Patients with rhinitis medicamentosa often experience recurrent or persistent nasal congestion, typically without rhinorrhea, due to prolonged and frequent use of intranasal decongestants. This severe congestion can lead to oral breathing, dry mouth, and snoring. During physical examination, the nasal mucosa typically appears swollen, red, and granular, although the mucosa may also appear pale and swollen.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;With disease progression, the nasal membrane&#x000a0;can become atrophic and crusted.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;The appearance of the nasal mucosa does not distinguish RM from infectious or allergic rhinitis.</p>
        <p>The evaluation should emphasize a thorough history of topical nasal decongestant use, including the specific agent, frequency, duration, and dosage, because this information is crucial for diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0; Both physiological and psychological dependence, along with addiction, can contribute to additional symptoms like headaches, restlessness, and anxiety, especially after discontinuation of nasal decongestants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;Physical examination should involve a thorough inspection of the nasal mucosa for signs of swelling and redness, as well as an evaluation for associated sinonasal conditions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Nasal examination may include anterior rhinoscopy and rigid or fiberoptic endoscopy, with or without video capabilities. Rhinitis medicamentosa is a diagnosis of exclusion, meaning clinicians must differentiate the condition from other causes of chronic nasal congestion. Specific features that distinguish rhinitis medicamentosa from&#x000a0;conditions such as allergic rhinitis (characterized by sneezing and nasal or ocular itching), chronic rhinosinusitis, structural abnormalities, and other forms of nonallergic rhinitis should be evaluated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>A diagnosis of RM is established primarily through clinical evaluation, as there are no definitive biochemical tests or imaging studies available to confirm the condition. Therefore, an accurate diagnosis requires a comprehensive assessment of the patient's symptoms and medical history, along with a thorough examination. Additionally, clinicians must recognize that other sinonasal conditions may coexist with RM. Special circumstances include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Skin testing for aeroallergens may further help differentiate allergic from nonallergic rhinitis when the diagnosis is unclear.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r14">[14]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Aspirin challenge and desensitization in patients with aspirin&#x02013;exacerbated respiratory disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r24">[24]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sinus imaging, such as a high-resolution CT scan, is used to rule out nasal polyps, rhinosinusitis, and anatomic abnormalities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r25">[25]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>The primary treatment for rhinitis medicamentosa is discontinuation of the topical nasal decongestant. Clinicians should advise patients that nasal congestion may temporarily worsen after stopping the medication; this rebound effect should not be mistaken for treatment failure. Intranasal corticosteroids have been shown to alleviate rebound congestion in animal studies and in several small human trials, and clinicians often use them during the withdrawal period.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r1">[1]</xref> Additional therapies may include short courses of oral corticosteroids (eg, prednisone at 0.5 mg/kg daily for 5 days), oral antihistamines, corticosteroid injections into the inferior turbinate, oral adenosine, and mast cell&#x02013;stabilizing agents.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Using nasal saline irrigation as an adjunctive measure may improve symptoms and reduce medication use, particularly for patients with frequent rhinitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r26">[26]</xref> Oral decongestants like pseudoephedrine can provide temporary relief during withdrawal, although supporting evidence is limited, and these agents may worsen symptoms. Patients should be advised of potential cardiovascular contraindications to decongestants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r26">[26]</xref> In clinical practice, gradual tapering strategies, such as discontinuing the topical decongestant in 1 nostril at a time or diluting the medication with saline, followed by slow withdrawal, have been shown&#x000a0;to be effective.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Nasal congestion should be assessed individually, taking into account accompanying signs and symptoms of nasal and paranasal disorders. While rhinitis and rhinosinusitis are the conditions most commonly associated with nasal congestion, clinicians should also recognize that other factors, such as anatomical abnormalities, can also contribute. The differential diagnosis for nasal congestion includes the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Rhinitis
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Infectious rhinitis
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Viral</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bacterial</p></list-item><list-item><p>Fungal&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r27">[27]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Allergic
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Seasonal</p></list-item><list-item><p>Perennial</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Occupational exposure</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hormonal</p></list-item><list-item><p>Toxins and pollution</p></list-item><list-item><p>Vasomotor (nonallergic)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r28">[28]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Drug-induced
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Aspirin</p></list-item><list-item><p>Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs</p></list-item><list-item><p>Centrally acting sympatholytics (eg, clonidine, methyldopa, reserpine, mecamylamine, prazosin, sildenafil, tadalafil, and others)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Antihypertensives (eg, amiloride, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, &#x003b2;-blockers, calcium channel blockers, chlorothiazide, hydralazine, hydrochlorothiazide, and others)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hormone therapy (eg, exogenous estrogens, oral contraceptives)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Psychotropics (eg, chlordiazepoxide, amitriptyline, chlorpromazine, risperidone, thioridazine)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Gabapentin</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rhinosinusitis
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>With and without nasal polyps</p></list-item><list-item><p>Acute</p></list-item><list-item><p>Recurrent acute</p></list-item><list-item><p>Chronic</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anatomic
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Deviated septum</p></list-item><list-item><p>Enlarged turbinates</p></list-item><list-item><p>Adenoidal hypertrophy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Choanal atresia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cleft palate&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r29">[29]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Autoimmune disease
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (formerly Wegener granulomatosis)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r30">[30]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Systemic lupus erythematosus</p></list-item><list-item><p>Sarcoidosis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r31">[31]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Rheumatoid arthritis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r32">[32]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Localized sinonasal amyloidosis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r33">[33]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Paranasal sinus cholesterol granulomata&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r34">[34]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (formally Churg&#x02013;Strauss syndrome), a rare granulomatous necrotizing small vessel vasculitis characterized by the presence of asthma, sinusitis, and hypereosinophilia&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r35">[35]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Rhinoscleroma (caused by <italic toggle="yes">Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis</italic> and a significant genetic component)<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r36">[36]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tumors
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Benign</p></list-item><list-item><p>Malignant</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Other causes
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Foreign body</p></list-item><list-item><p>Intranasal narcotic abuse
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Cocaine-induced midline destructive lesions</p></list-item><list-item><p><italic toggle="yes">Aspergillus flavus </italic>infection after using cocaine&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r37">[37]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Continuous positive airway pressure or bilevel airway positive airway pressure</p></list-item><list-item><p>Nasal cannula</p></list-item><list-item><p>Immunocompromise</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The key to successful treatment depends largely on prevention and patient education. Rhinitis medicamentosa typically demonstrates a favorable prognosis when managed appropriately, particularly with discontinuation of the offending topical decongestant. Symptoms generally improve following cessation of the causative agent and initiation of supportive therapy. However, clinicians should advise patients with rhinitis medicamentosa against reusing topical decongestants, even for short durations, because rebound congestion may recur rapidly with repeated exposure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>The recovery timeline depends on the treatment approach used. Subjective symptoms of rebound congestion may resolve within 48 hours when intranasal corticosteroids are initiated alongside discontinuation of the decongestant, whereas resolution may take longer than one week with placebo.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;In contrast, objective mucosal abnormalities, such as edema and inflammation, may take longer to resolve, often requiring 7 to 14 days of treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;These findings suggest that although acute withdrawal symptoms may resolve relatively quickly, full mucosal recovery and optimal symptom management might necessitate a longer treatment period.</p>
        <p>For patients with chronic rhinitis treated with intranasal corticosteroids, achieving maximum therapeutic benefit may take several weeks. However, initial clinical improvement is often seen within the first day of treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r40">[40]</xref>&#x000a0;These findings indicate that although acute withdrawal symptoms may resolve relatively quickly, full mucosal recovery and optimal symptom management might necessitate a longer treatment period.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Patients with RM face psychological and physical dependence, which may lead to anxiety and withdrawal symptoms. Complications may include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Structural nasal and paranasal damage&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r38">[38]</xref>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Mucosal hyperplasia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Chronic rhinosinusitis with and without nasal polyps</p></list-item><list-item><p>Atrophic rhinitis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r41">[41]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Septal perforation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r42">[42]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Increased intraoperative bleeding&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r43">[43]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Systemic cardiovascular effects such as hypertension&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s13" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>In complex cases or when the diagnosis is unclear, further expert input from an allergist, immunologist, or otolaryngologist is recommended.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patients should be educated on the potential adverse effects of prolonged or inappropriate use of intranasal topical decongestants, ideally before initiation of therapy. Education is&#x000a0;particularly&#x000a0;important because many of these medications are readily available over the counter and may be perceived as harmless. Patients should be counseled that repeated short-term use of topical decongestants, even after prolonged discontinuation, can lead to recurrence of rhinitis medicamentosa.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28519.r44">[44]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients should be informed that nasal congestion may temporarily worsen after discontinuing a decongestant. This rebound effect may be expected during the treatment. Providing clear guidance on the anticipated recovery process can help prevent patients from prematurely resuming decongestant use and improve their adherence to the therapy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Rhinitis medicamentosa is largely preventable, and effective management is essential to reduce patient morbidity and prevent recurrence. Early recognition, coupled with appropriate intervention, is critical to achieve these goals. A collaborative, interdisciplinary approach is necessary, involving primary care clinicians, otolaryngologists, allergists, pharmacists, nurses, and other health care professionals.</p>
        <p>Clinicians must be adept at identifying rhinitis medicamentosa in patients presenting with persistent nasal congestion and distinguishing it from other forms of rhinitis and other nasal and paranasal sinus abnormalities. Pharmacists are instrumental in identifying patterns of overuse of over-the-counter topical decongestants and play a vital role in educating patients about safe usage durations. Additionally, nurses and advanced practitioners contribute by reinforcing proper medication use, monitoring symptom progression, and supporting adherence throughout the withdrawal process. Patient education regarding potential rebound symptoms and preventive strategies is essential for successful treatment. A coordinated, team-based approach enhances diagnostic accuracy, facilitates the safe discontinuation of topical decongestants, and minimizes the risk of relapse.</p>
        <p>Effective interdisciplinary communication is crucial for accurately collecting medication histories and ensuring consistent counseling messages across care settings. This coordination is particularly important during the withdrawal phase, because patients may be inclined to resume decongestant use in response to temporary symptom exacerbation. By integrating evidence-based management strategies, prioritizing patient education, and fostering shared responsibility among health care team members, interprofessional collaboration can significantly enhance patient outcomes and alleviate the burden of RM.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28519.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
